<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Persis</h1><p class="st-meta">260 BCE – 224 CE (approx.) · capital Istakhr</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The local kings of Fars, heartland of the Persians, who kept Achaemenid memory alive under Greek and Parthian overlords until one of them founded the Sasanian Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Alexander's conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, but in the 3rd century BCE local dynasts called frataraka ('governor' or 'leader') began issuing their own coins from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagadates-i">Bagadates</a> is often seen as the first independent frataraka, and his successors broke away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The frataraka coins show the ruler before a fire altar or Achaemenid-style monuments, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> legends, signalling continuity with the Persian past and Zoroastrian worship. From the mid-2nd century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, and its rulers took the title king (shah). Kings such as Darayan and Manchihr bore names drawn from Iranian epic. The land lived from agriculture in the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kor-river">Kur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulvar-river">Pulvar</a> rivers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 205 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papak">Pabag</a>, a local lord at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>, seized power, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir</a> made himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>. In 224 CE Ardashir defeated the Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-persis.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Persis" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Persis:HistoryofIran Background:Natural Earth. Free vector and raster map data @ naturalearthdata.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_domain_of_the_Kings_of_Persis_(geographical).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>260 BCE – 224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Istakhr</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bagadates, Vadfradad I, Pabag</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Persian, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Persis, Frataraka, Kings of Persis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-18&amp;focus=kingdom-of-persis" aria-label="Kingdom of Persis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6055 1083 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Persis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6375 1339L6140 1232M6375 1339L6141 1232M6375 1339L6357 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1232h0M6141 1232h0M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6375 1339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6375 1339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Istakhr. <a href="/atlas?year=-18&amp;focus=kingdom-of-persis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
